     which stops it SHRINKING, but content longer than the original still grows
     it (measured 80px -> 94px without this). */
  .mcp-endpoint__copy {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    min-width: 6rem;
  }

  /* `display: block` is load-bearing, and only ONE of these two rows shows why.
     The Endpoint row's <code> is a direct child of the flex/grid parent, so it
     is BLOCKIFIED automatically and paints one box. The Status row's <code> is
     wrapped in an <a> (it is meant to be visited, not just copied), so the
     ANCHOR is the item that gets blockified and the <code> inside it stays
     `display: inline`.

     An inline box that wraps paints one fragment per line — border-left plus
     top/bottom on the first, border-right plus top/bottom on the last — so at
     phone widths the Status URL's border tore in half across two lines while
     the Endpoint's, identical in every declared style, did not. Measured at
     430px before the fix: Endpoint 1 client rect, Status 2. Nothing overflowed
     the row or the page; the box simply fragmented.

     "Identical in every declared style" was true of the CSS and NOT of the
     rendering, at any width. The two boxes also differed by 3px of height at
     desktop — Status 41px against Endpoint 44px, same x and same width —
     because an inline box is sized by font metrics where a block box is sized
     by line-height plus padding and border. So this rule fixes two things: a
     visible fragmentation at phone widths, and a silent 3px discrepancy at
     desktop that nobody had reported. Blockifying lands Status on exactly the
     44px Endpoint already had.

     Declaring it here rather than on the anchor keeps the two rows' <code>
     boxes governed by one rule, so wrapping a future value in a link cannot
     reintroduce this. */
  .mcp-endpoint code {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-family-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
